  "benchmark": "greenfield-dram-design",
  "expectedEffect": "In the exact production-window case, reduce final source-lot [dram-lot-08] packaged WIP from 8 to 0 and raise delivered devices from 88 to 96 while retaining 12 scheduled/released/completed/on-time wafer lots, zero scrap and quality escapes, and no worse contract fulfillment. The comparison must report the added rack build-cost, occupied-area, energy, utilization, and any interruption-case regression; KEEP is not implied if those costs dominate the recovered memory.",
  "hypothesis": "Add one explicitly costed qualified DRAM burn-in rack as a parallel overflow service path, with package input, commercial/performance/automotive outputs, power, and the same authored full-batch screening programs; do not change the twelve-lot Production Plan, release cadence, Process batch sizes, incumbent dispatch policy, or customer contracts. Because the complete dram-lot-08 batch is resident 30450 ticks before the incumbent becomes free, parallel service should expose whether back-end capacity rather than external release timing owns the terminal tail.",
